designers.caveats:
1. Tie-break proxy. tm_log_inst (inst_total) is used as a raster tie-breaker. It is legal under the current contract but is a codegen-artifact proxy for cfg.ext swizzle type. It holds on the a6000 bundle (hz has fewer instructions in 92% of configs and never more) and may not transfer.
2. Design exposure. Designs were explored on fold-0 train shapes, which include 3/4 of the other folds' holdouts. featdiag and my 4-fold surrogate screen used every fold's train shapes, so the design has seen all 65 shapes' times in train roles. No holdout results or holdout regrets were read, and only 2 probe --dev runs were used.
3. Choices informed by the data (no fitted constants inside features):
   - p=2 norm form;
   - SM-granular waves;
   - L2 priced at DRAM bandwidth in the headline feature (a bound; the rule fits kappa);
   - rule literals 1e-4/1e-3 (tie-break size) and 10.0 (time scale);
   - w0 priors for the tie-break sign (+1 for M>N, -1 for M<=N) and kappa (0.5);
   - tm_cta_warps uses the warp width 32 (lanes per warp, not a GPU value).
4. Big class remains worse than the vendor in inner-CV (1.085 vs 1.032). On 8192-row grids, 128x128 kernels at swizzle 1 are 1.3x slower than 256x128 even though my raster/L2 models predict identical DRAM traffic. Visible physics does not explain it; a big-grid branch did not help in the surrogate (overfits with few big shapes).
5. Duplicates. Under the loop's section 8.4 check, tm_res_inv (vs resident_block_scarcity) and tm_reduce_ratio (vs parallel_reduction_compute_fraction) would be refused. All 10 load with load_generated; the other 8 pass full section 8.3 validation.
6. Surrogate optimism. The surrogate numbers use a softmin L-BFGS fitter and are about 0.005 more optimistic than the probe's CMA. Only the two dev runs are official.
7. Weight stability. Fold weights vary widely (e.g. w on log-time 1.8-33 in tm_A), so the regret landscape is flat. Only kappa, the tie-break sign, tm_cta_warps and tm_regstaged were stable.
